K42 = S("S1.4.4.2",
  "A binary row addition $y_{j_i}\\leftarrow y_{j_i}\\oplus x_{j_i}$ on the same bit position in $f$ address chunks of width $K$ at once, with a third chunk $z$ restored, costs $O(V(L^\\tau+1))$ with $L=fK$, the few exceptional addresses being repaired deterministically.",
  "Realize the conditional parity flip by eight conditional additions, each a single cyclic shift of a whole chunk simultaneously on all segment pairs; segments cannot carry into each other away from a small explicit set $\\mathcal B$ of addresses, which is extracted, stably radix-sorted by destination rank and reinserted.",
  M("The construction first uses a constant number of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, then corrects an explicitly bounded set of addresses, and the correction depends only on addresses, so the bound is worst case over all payloads.", "explicit",
    "of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, then corrects an explicitly bounded set of"),
  [
    I("S1.4.4.2.1", "For a Boolean control $c$, the eight additions (1) to (8) of $u$ and $w$ do nothing if $c=0$, and if $c=1$ the first four induce the cycle $(00\\ 01\\ 10)$ on parities of $(u,w)$ in every square $\\{2a,2a+1\\}\\times\\{2b,2b+1\\}$ and the last four induce $(01\\ 10\\ 11)$.",
      "If $c=0$, these operations do nothing. If $c=1$, the first four operations"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.2", "Applying the first cycle and then the second gives $(00\\ 10)(01\\ 11)$, which flips the parity of $u$ and fixes $w$.",
      "induce $(01\\ 10\\ 11)$. Applying the first cycle and then the second gives"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.3", "Hence $(u',w')=(u+c(1-2(u\\bmod2)),w)$: the parity of $u$ flips exactly when $c=1$, both quotients by two are preserved, and the auxiliary integer $w$ is restored; each coordinate moves by at most four.",
      "\\bigl(u+c(1-2(u\\bmod2)),\\ w\\bigr)."),
    I("S1.4.4.2.4", "Each line applied simultaneously to the $f-1$ disjoint $K$-bit segment pairs is one cyclic shift $y\\leftarrow y+\\varepsilon\\sum_i2^{j_i}[x_{j_i}=1][z_{j_i}=b]\\pmod{2^L}$ whose offset never depends on the target chunk, hence a bijection of the rectangle.",
      "Apply each line of \\eqref{eq:eight-packed-additions} simultaneously to all"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.5", "Away from the bad set $\\mathcal B$ of addresses where some used guard value fails $10\\le g\\le2^{K-1}-11$, each segment lies in $[20,2^K-21]$ and a displacement of eight keeps it inside $[0,2^K-1]$, so no segment carries into or borrows from the next and $S_0$ agrees with the ideal XOR permutation $T_0$.",
      "At an address outside $\\mathcal B$, each segment initially lies in"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.6", "Each guard has exactly twenty excluded values out of $2^{K-1}$ and there are $2(f-1)$ guards, so by the union bound $|\\mathcal B|\\le\\delta_{\\rm bad}M$ with $\\delta_{\\rm bad}=\\min\\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\\}$, independently of the record payload.",
      "There are exactly twenty excluded values for each guard in"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.7", "$S_0$ and $T_0$ are bijections agreeing off $\\mathcal B$, so both preserve $\\mathcal B$ and $T_0S_0^{-1}$ fixes $\\mathcal B^c$ and permutes $\\mathcal B$.",
      "Thus $T_0S_0^{-1}$ fixes $\\mathcal B^c$ and permutes $\\mathcal B$."),
    I("S1.4.4.2.8", "Each of the at most sixteen swaps and eight rotations of $S_0$ costs $O(VL^\\tau)$ by the chunk interchange, the rotation itself being a controlled shift costing $O(V)$ since its offset is computed in $L^{O(1)}$ time from the two control values.",
      "descriptor has $O(A_{\\rm rect})$ bits. The swap costs $O(VL^\\tau)$, including"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.9", "The correction extracts the records of $\\mathcal B$ with destination rank keys, stably radix sorts only the extracted records by key, and reinserts them in one forward scan; since $T_0S_0^{-1}$ permutes $\\mathcal B$ the keys are distinct and exactly the ranks of the marked holes.",
      "while stable binary radix sorting scans only the extracted records and"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.10", "The omitted most significant selected position is handled by a two-bit XOR at cost $O(V)$ (for $f=1$, $S_0,T_0$ are identities, $\\mathcal B$ is empty and this step is the whole algorithm).",
      "Finally apply the two-bit XOR to $x_{j_{f-1}},y_{j_{f-1}}$ by"),
    I("S1.4.4.2.11", "After division by $V=MR$ the extra terms are bounded by $A_{\\rm rect}^3/R$ and $80(f-1)2^{-K}A_{\\rm rect}(1+A_{\\rm rect}/R)$, which are $o(1)$ when $A_{\\rm rect}=O(p)$, $R$ exceeds every fixed polynomial in $p$, and $K/\\log p\\to\\infty$, leaving $O(V(L^\\tau+1))$.",
      "$O(p^2 2^{-K}(1+A_{\\rm rect}/R))=o(1)$. This proves the final assertion."),
    I("S1.4.4.2.12", "A fixed binary change of basis, a product of a fixed number of row additions, and its inverse therefore cost $O(V((eK)^\\tau+1))$ on a stream of volume $V$.",
      "the complete change of basis and its inverse therefore cost"),
  ],
  page="packed-selected-bit-addition",
  frm=["address-chunk-interchange", "controlled-shift", "elementary-stream-ops", "tape-address-model", "binary-representation-and-bit-operations", "sorting-and-data-movement-on-tapes", "modular-arithmetic", "finite-field-f2-arithmetic"],
  salient="Two three-cycles on the parity pairs compose to the transposition pair $(00\\ 10)(01\\ 11)$, which flips the parity of $u$ and returns the arbitrary auxiliary integer $w$ unchanged: a conditional XOR is obtained from additions alone, and additions on all $f$ segments at once are a single cyclic shift of a whole chunk.",
  adds=[B("f", "chunk count", "The number of address chunks whose selected bit position is changed simultaneously.", "count"),
        B("K", "chunk width", "The number of bits in each address chunk; segments of $K$ bits carry the packed additions.", "count"),
        B("\\mathcal B", "bad set", "The set of addresses at which some guard value of a $K$-bit segment lies outside the safe interval, and the packed shifts may carry.", "direct")])

K43 = S("S1.4.4.3",
  "For $e$ active chunks the complex network gives the recurrence $F(e)\\le(s/W)F(e/m)+O((eK)^\\tau+1)$ with $F(e)=O(e)$ for $e<d^\\beta$, and unrolling it gives cost $O(d^{\\lambda'})$ per unit of volume for a layer on $D\\le d$ chunks.",
  "Split each node on $e=m^k$ chunks into $m$ slots with $s$ calls on $e/m$ chunks, supply the rows from the first $q_0$ chunks, deal them to $W$ role streams of volume $V/W$, add the node overhead from the packed basis changes, and sum the recurrence in three parts: overhead, internal nodes and leaves.",
  M("The overhead of a node is the packed change of basis on slots of $(e/m)K$ address bits, which costs $O(V((eK)^\\tau+1))$, so the recurrence is that of the bit case with a polynomially larger overhead.", "recoverable"),
  [
    I("S1.4.4.3.1", "A node on $e=m^k$ chunks makes exactly $s$ calls on slots of $e/m$ chunks, and the binary basis changes, scalar gates, endpoint corrections and reassembly cost $O(V((eK)^\\tau+1))$ at the node.",
      "The phase construction supplies one recursive call on a slot for each"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.2", "Exception: if $D\\le q_0$ the whole layer is done by individual $C$ kernels at cost $O(V\\log d)$.",
      "If \\(D\\le q_0\\), use individual \\(C\\) kernels, at cost \\(O(V\\log d)\\)."),
    I("S1.4.4.3.3", "Otherwise the first $q_0$ chunks are processed individually and their concatenated bits serve as a row index of range $R_{\\rm row}=2^{q_0K}\\ge W^{k_0}$; padding rows to the next multiple of $W^{k_0}$ at most doubles the volume.",
      "Otherwise apply \\(C\\) individually on the selected bits of the first"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.4", "Writing a row index as $u=Wg+w$ and sending the whole row to wire role $w$ gives role streams of identical address shape, so each recursive edge call acts on one role stream of exactly $1/W$ of its parent's logical volume.",
      "one role stream with volume exactly \\(1/W\\) of its parent's logical"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.5", "Padded zero rows are again zero when an invocation finishes, and the invocations commute with the earlier individual layers because their selected bits lie in disjoint chunks, so the added rows can be deleted at the end.",
      "Each padded zero row is again zero when an invocation finishes, so the"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.6", "With $\\beta=1/2$, stopping when $e<d^\\beta$ and applying $e$ kernels individually, the normalized time satisfies $F(e)\\le(s/W)F(e/m)+O((eK)^\\tau+1)$ and $F(e)=O(e)$ at leaves, because the $s$ child calls run on streams of volume $V/W$.",
      "F(e)\\le (s/W)F(e/m)+O((eK)^\\tau+1),"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.7", "At an internal node $e\\ge d^\\beta$, so $K\\le d^c\\le e^{c/\\beta}$ and the overhead is $O(e^{\\tau(1+c/\\beta)}+1)=O(e^\\lambda)$ whenever $\\tau(1+c/\\beta)<\\lambda$.",
      "\\(K\\le d^c\\le e^{c/\\beta}\\).  The overhead is therefore"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.8", "With $a=s/W\\le m^\\sigma<m^\\lambda$ the internal costs sum to at most $Ce^\\lambda\\sum_{j\\ge0}(a/m^\\lambda)^j=O(e^\\lambda)$, a convergent geometric series since $a/m^\\lambda<1$.",
      "C e^\\lambda\\sum_{j\\ge0}(a/m^\\lambda)^j=O(e^\\lambda)."),
    I("S1.4.4.3.9", "Leaves with $u=e/m^j<d^\\beta$ chunks after $j$ levels contribute at most $O(a^ju)\\le O(e^\\sigma u^{1-\\sigma})\\le O(e^\\sigma d^{\\beta(1-\\sigma)})$ using $a^j\\le m^{j\\sigma}=(e/u)^\\sigma$, which also covers $a<1$ by taking $\\sigma\\ge0$.",
      "O(a^ju)\\le O(e^\\sigma u^{1-\\sigma})"),
    I("S1.4.4.3.10", "A general $D-q_0=\\sum_ja_jm^j$ in base $m$ with $0\\le a_j<m$ is partitioned into at most $(m-1)(1+\\lfloor\\log_md\\rfloor)$ consecutive pieces of $m^j$ chunks.",
      "Write \\(D-q_0=\\sum_j a_jm^j\\) in base \\(m\\), with \\(0\\le a_j<m\\)."),
    I("S1.4.4.3.11", "Adding the pieces and the row preprocessing gives $O(\\log(2d)[d^\\lambda+d^{\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)}+1])=O(d^{\\lambda'})$ for any fixed $\\max\\{\\lambda,\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)\\}<\\lambda'<1$, the strict inequality absorbing the logarithmic number of groups.",
      "\\bigl[d^\\lambda+d^{\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)}+1\\bigr]\\right)"),
  ],
  frm=["packed-selected-bit-addition", "phase-frame-kernel", "rank-saving-recurrence", "address-chunk-interchange", "recurrences-and-induction"],
  salient="The overhead exponent is $\\tau(1+c/\\beta)<\\lambda<1$: the chunk-swap exponent applies to the address width $eK$ rather than to $e$, and $K=\\lfloor d^c\\rfloor$ with tiny $c$ keeps the total exponent below one.",
  adds=[B("\\beta", "stopping exponent", "The exponent $1/2$ such that recursion stops when the number of chunks $e$ falls below $d^\\beta$.", "direct"),
        B("\\lambda", "overhead exponent", "A fixed exponent in $(\\max\\{\\tau,\\sigma\\},1)$ bounding the normalized node overhead $O(e^\\lambda)$.", "direct")])

K44 = S("S1.4.4.4",
  "The intermediate Gaussian dyadics of one exactly evaluated layer need only $\\Delta=\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil$ extra fractional and integer bits, which is $o(p)$, so the coefficient width stays $O(p)$.",
  "Bound the arithmetic dependency depth $A(e)$ along any chain from an input coefficient to an intermediate one by a recurrence that concatenates the $s$ child calls, then convert depth into denominator and magnitude growth.",
  M("Each addition at most doubles a modulus and each halving adds one denominator bit, so the number of operations on any chain bounds both growth of magnitude and of denominator.", "recoverable"),
  [
    I("S1.4.4.4.1", "The depth satisfies $A(e)\\le sA(e/m)+E$ at an internal node and $A(e)\\le8e$ at a leaf, where $E=64(W+m+1)^3$ bounds the scalar gates ($24W^3$), child corrections ($4s$) and bank corrections.",
      "A(e)\\le sA(e/m)+E"),
    I("S1.4.4.4.2", "The depth recurrence uses $s$ and not $s/W$ because arithmetic chains concatenate all $s$ calls, whereas the time recurrence has the factor $1/W$ from smaller stream volumes.",
      "The depth estimate concatenates the \\(s\\) calls, so it uses \\(s\\)."),
    I("S1.4.4.4.3", "For any bound $x\\ge1$ one has $sx+E\\le(s+E)x=Bx$ with $B=s+E$, so through at most $k\\le\\lceil\\log_md\\rceil$ internal levels starting from $8e\\le8dB$ at a leaf one gets $A(e)\\le8dB^{k+1}$.",
      "For any bound \\(x\\ge1\\), \\(sx+E\\le(s+E)x=Bx\\)."),
    I("S1.4.4.4.4", "Since $B\\le m^\\nu$ and $B^{k+1}\\le B^{\\log_md+2}=B^2d^{\\log_mB}\\le B^2d^\\nu$, the depth is $A(e)\\le8B^2d^{\\nu+1}$.",
      "A(e)\\le 8dB^{k+1}\\le8B^2d^{\\nu+1}."),
    I("S1.4.4.4.5", "Adding the at most $md$ pieces, the individual kernels and the outer diagonals and scalar $b^D$ gives a total below $128mB^2d^{\\nu+3}\\le C_0d^{C_1}$, with $C_1=\\max\\{20,\\nu+3\\}=20$.",
      "<128mB^2d^{\\nu+3}\\le C_0d^{C_1}."),
    I("S1.4.4.4.6", "A value at depth $\\ell$ lies in $2^{-(p+\\ell)}\\mathbb Z[i]$ and has modulus at most $2^\\ell$ by induction (addition at most doubles the modulus, halving adds one denominator bit), so reserving $p+\\Delta$ fractional and $\\Delta+3$ integer bits represents every intermediate coefficient exactly.",
      "\\(2^\\ell\\).  This follows by induction: addition or subtraction"),
    I("S1.4.4.4.7", "Choosing $\\epsilon C_1<1$ with $d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon)$ gives $\\Delta=o(p)$, so the full coefficient width remains $O(p)$.",
      "Choose \\(\\epsilon C_1<1\\).  Since \\(d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon)\\),"),
  ],
  frm=["fixed-point-truncation", "norms-absolute-values-and-error-bounds", "recurrences-and-induction"],
  salient="The guard width is a power of $d$ (about $d^{20}$) and $d=\\Theta(p^{\\epsilon})$ with $\\epsilon=2^{-75}$, so $20\\epsilon<1$ makes it $o(p)$, even though no intermediate operator in the layer is a contraction.",
  adds=[B("\\Delta", "guard bits", "The number $\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil$ of extra fractional bits (and integer bits) reserved so that all dyadic intermediates are exact.", "count"),
        B("A(e)", "dependency depth", "The maximum number of complex additions, subtractions, halvings, sign changes and multiplications by $i$ on a chain from an input coefficient to an intermediate one.", "count")])

